---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import SubstitutionLab from "../../components/guides/SubstitutionLab";
import { encodedGroup } from "../../data/substitutionPractice";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");
---

<BaseLayout title="Break substitution ciphers with linked clues">
  <header class="wrap page-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">GUIDE 04 / LINK THE CLUES</p>
    <h1>
      Guess a group.
      <br />
      Unlock more letters<span class="accent">.</span>
    </h1>
    <p>
      Use THE, THA and HER as a chain of clues. Test each guess across the message and keep a way
      back.
    </p>
  </header>
  <div class="wrap article-layout">
    <nav class="article-toc" aria-label="Guide contents">
      <a href="#groups">01 / Start with groups</a>
      <a href="#chain">02 / THE → THA → HER</a>
      <a href="#practice">03 / Practise the chain</a>
      <a href="#habits">04 / Work faster</a>
    </nav>
    <article class="guide-content">
      <h2 id="groups">A group gives you several letters at once</h2>
      <p>
        In a simple substitution, each ciphertext letter always means the same plaintext letter. The
        most common single letter may suggest e, but many guesses fit just one count. A{" "}
        <strong>bigram</strong> (two letters) or <strong>trigram</strong> (three letters) gives you
        linked evidence that is easier to check.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>THE</strong> is a common English trigram; <strong>TH</strong> and{" "}
        <strong>HE</strong> are common bigrams, and <strong>E</strong> is a common letter. If a
        frequent cipher triple begins with a frequent pair and ends with a frequent letter, THE is a
        promising hypothesis. Enter all three guesses and read every place they appear.
      </p>
      <aside class="guide-callout">
        <strong>Common does not mean certain.</strong> THE, TH and E lead large reference
        collections, but they need not lead your passage. HE can be more common than TH; names or a
        repeated phrase can dominate. THA and HER are useful common groups, not guaranteed winners.
        Keep a guess because it explains several clues.
      </aside>
      <h2 id="chain">Build the chain: THE → THA → HER</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Test THE.</strong> In our example, <code>{encodedGroup("THE")}</code> is the
          leading trigram. Try it as <code>the</code>. Its first two cipher letters should now read{" "}
          <code>th</code> everywhere, and its last two should read <code>he</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Find th plus one unknown.</strong> Look for <code>{encodedGroup("THA")}</code>,
          which will partly read <code>th{encodedGroup("A")}</code>. Try THA, a group found in THAT
          and THAN. That gives you an a candidate. Does it improve several words?
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Find he plus one unknown.</strong> <code>{encodedGroup("HER")}</code> will partly
          read <code>he{encodedGroup("R")}</code>. Try HER for r. It occurs inside THERE, WHERE,
          OTHER and TOGETHER as well as the word HER.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Use the new letters to test longer words.</strong> If several places now suggest
          THERE or THAT, the guesses support each other. If they make impossible patterns, undo the
          latest guess and try another.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        This chain gives you five plaintext letters from three overlapping groups. You are building
        one consistent alphabet; you cannot decide that a cipher letter means e in one place and a
        somewhere else.
      </p>
      <h2 id="practice">Try it on an original practice message</h2>
      <p>
        Start without word spaces, as you often will in a challenge. Choose a group from the table,
        type a plaintext hypothesis and test it. The hint for each step is optional. You can undo a
        complete guess or clear one letter.
      </p>
      <SubstitutionLab client:load />
      <h2 id="habits">Habits that save time</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Change one idea at a time.</strong> Check the new letters across the whole message
          before adding more. Undo records your route back.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Match repeated-letter patterns.</strong> THERE repeats its third letter in
          position five. LETTER repeats its second letter in position five, and has a double letter
          in positions three and four. A candidate must fit the exact pattern.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Check both directions.</strong> In a simple substitution one cipher letter has one
          plaintext partner, and two different cipher letters cannot both mean e. The practice tool
          catches these contradictions.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Look beyond the top group.</strong> AND, ING, ION, ENT, THA and HER offer more
          starting points. ING may be a word ending, but display blocks do not mark words.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Question your cipher type.</strong> If a consistent mapping keeps failing, revisit{" "}
          <a href={`${base}/guides/identify/`}>the identification guide</a>. A transposition or
          Vigenère cipher needs a different approach.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="button primary" href={`${base}/tools/#ngrams`}>
          Inspect groups in your own text ↗
        </a>
        <a class="text-link" href={`${base}/tools/#substitution`}>
          Enter your substitutions →
        </a>
      </div>
      <p class="small">
        The teaching passage, ciphertext and worked chain are original to this site and deliberately
        emphasise these groups. Reference ranks:{" "}
        <a href="https://www.norvig.com/mayzner.html">
          Peter Norvig, English Letter Frequency Counts
        </a>{" "}
        (within-word counts, whereas our analysis removes spaces and includes cross-word groups).
        The THE/THA/HER practice sequence is our teaching example; its exact ranks are not a
        prediction for other messages. For the general method, see{" "}
        <a href="https://cs.uri.edu/cryptography/classical-ciphers/substitution/cryptanalysis-demo.html">
          University of Rhode Island’s substitution cryptanalysis demonstration
        </a>
        .
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
